  "slides": [
    {
      "slide_id": "cover",
      "slide_role": "unit_cover",
      "heading": "Conceptual Spaces and Creative Change",
      "sentence": "Every style is a space with rules. You can combine inside it, explore it, or change one of its rules.",
      "quote": "Creativity is the ability to come up with ideas or artefacts that are new, surprising and valuable.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Boden 2004, 1)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/conceptual-spaces-creative-change/#ref-boden-2004"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "definition-of-creativity",
          "conceptual-spaces"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text"
        ]
      },
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Creativity is the ability to come up with ideas or artefacts that are new, surprising and valuable.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 1"
        }
      ],
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "image_brief": "One style written down as a grammar: many patterns, same rules — a conceptual space on a single page.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Owen Jones - Grammar of Ornament - 1868 - plate 043 - 300ppi.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-CS.cover"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "analysis-opener",
      "slide_role": "analysis_opener",
      "background_kind": "geometrical",
      "heading": "Read the style",
      "sentence": "Before theory: pick one piece of design. What are its rules? Which rule held, and which one changed?",
      "background_url": "/assets/images/fractal-pass-track/ct-pass-01-structure-980beb83.svg"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "analysis-model",
      "slide_role": "analysis_model",
      "heading": "Language · medium · support",
      "sentence": "Language: the style and its rules. Medium: the material, which has rules too. Support: where the work lives, and who decides it is valuable.",
      "quote": "many arguments about creativity are rooted in disagreements about value.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Boden 2004, 10)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/conceptual-spaces-creative-change/#ref-boden-2004"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "analysis",
          "value",
          "circulation"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text"
        ]
      },
      "notes": "- Language = the style's grammar; today we call it a conceptual space.\n- Medium = material and technique: it makes some moves easy, others nearly impossible.\n- Support = magazine, shop, feed, jury: who gets to call it valuable.\n- Then: which road did the piece take — combine, explore or transform? Name the rule.\nSource: (Boden 2004, 10)\nAsk: Who decided this 1880 dress was in fashion — the maker, the magazine, or the buyer?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "many arguments about creativity are rooted in disagreements about value.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 10"
        }
      ],
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "image_brief": "A fashion plate to read with language, medium and support: the style, the print, and the magazine that circulated it.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:La Mode Illustrée, 1880, No. 22 Toilettes de Mon. Fladry, RP-P-2009-3683.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-CS.analysis-model"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-1",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "heading": "1 · New, surprising, valuable — new to whom?",
      "sentence": "P-creative: new to you. H-creative: new to history. For a student, P-creativity is the one that counts.",
      "quote": "P–creativity involves coming up with a surprising, valuable idea that’s new to the person who comes up with it.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Boden 2004, 2)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/conceptual-spaces-creative-change/#ref-boden-2004"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "p-creativity",
          "h-creativity"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text"
        ]
      },
      "notes": "- New, surprising, valuable: all three.\n- Everyone is creative to a degree — not a rare gift.\n- Ask how creative, and in which way, not yes or no.\nSource: (Boden 2004, 1–2)\nAsk: Name an idea you had that turned out to be old. Did it stop being creative for you?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "P–creativity involves coming up with a surprising, valuable idea that’s new to the person who comes up with it.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 2"
        },
        {
          "text": "every one of us is creative, to a degree.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 1"
        },
        {
          "text": "Just how creative is it, and in just which way(s)?",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 2"
        }
      ],
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "image_brief": "A young person absorbed in an ordinary craft — an idea can be new to her even if it is old to history.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Albert Anker - Strickendes Mädchen (1888).jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-CS.masterclass-1"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-2",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "heading": "2 · A style is a space you can map",
      "sentence": "A conceptual space is a style of thinking with rules — in music, science, or couture. You pick it up from your culture.",
      "quote": "Conceptual spaces are structured styles of thought.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Boden 2004, 4)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/conceptual-spaces-creative-change/#ref-boden-2004"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "conceptual-spaces",
          "style"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text"
        ]
      },
      "notes": "- Boden's list includes fashions in couture and choreography.\n- No single person invents a space; you learn it from a group.\n- Later she calls these maps of the mind: they guide thought into some paths and not others.\nSource: (Boden 2004, 4; 59)\nAsk: Write three rules of a style everyone in this room would recognise.",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Conceptual spaces are structured styles of thought.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 4"
        },
        {
          "text": "generative systems that guide thought and action into some paths but not others.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 59"
        }
      ],
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "image_brief": "A road map with main roads and side roads — the map metaphor for a conceptual space you can explore.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:John Ogilby - The Road from London to the City of Bristol (1675).jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-CS.masterclass-2"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-3",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "heading": "3 · Three roads: combine, explore, transform",
      "sentence": "Combine familiar ideas in a new way. Explore what a style can already do. Or transform it by changing a rule.",
      "quote": "The first involves making unfamiliar combinations of familiar ideas.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Boden 2004, 3)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/conceptual-spaces-creative-change/#ref-boden-2004"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "combinational-creativity",
          "exploratory-creativity",
          "transformational-creativity"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text"
        ]
      },
      "notes": "- Combination needs a link people can follow; random is not enough.\n- Exploration shows possibilities you had not glimpsed.\n- Transformation makes the 'impossible' idea possible by changing the style.\nSource: (Boden 2004, 3–6)\nAsk: This quilt — which road? What would a transformed quilt look like?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The first involves making unfamiliar combinations of familiar ideas.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 3"
        },
        {
          "text": "Exploratory creativity is valuable because it can enable someone to see possibilities they hadn’t glimpsed before.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 4"
        },
        {
          "text": "The supposedly impossible idea can come about only if the creator changes the pre-existing style in some way.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 6"
        }
      ],
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "image_brief": "Familiar scraps joined into an unfamiliar whole — combination as textile collage.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Crazy Quilt MET DP171162.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-CS.masterclass-3"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-4",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "heading": "4 · Rules are the material of change",
      "sentence": "No rules means no style to change. Negate a rule, drop it, or ask what it is for. You can only break a rule you know.",
      "quote": "far from being the antithesis of creativity, constraints on thinking are what make it possible.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Boden 2004, 95)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/master-lectures/conceptual-spaces-creative-change/#ref-boden-2004"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "constraints",
          "consider-the-negative",
          "rule-change"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text"
        ]
      },
      "notes": "- Consider the negative: applied to a deep rule, it can redraw the space in one step.\n- Schoenberg dropped even the home-chord ending and left tonality.\n- Perkins: ask not how it is usually done but what it is supposed to do.\n- To recognise a structural novelty, you need a structured mind.\nSource: (Boden 2004, 66; 72; 74; 95), (Perkins 1995, 103)\nAsk: Which rule of your current project have you never questioned?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "far from being the antithesis of creativity, constraints on thinking are what make it possible.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 95"
        },
        {
          "text": "One very general heuristic is consider the negative.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 66"
        },
        {
          "text": "To recognize a structural novelty, one needs a structured mind.",
          "cite": "Boden 2004, 74"
        },
        {
          "text": "asking not how is this usually done but what is this supposed to do",
          "cite": "Perkins 1995, 103"
        }
      ],
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "image_brief": "An open chain turned into a closed ring — one rule changed, and a new family of shapes becomes thinkable.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Benzene-1866.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-CS.masterclass-4"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-5",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "heading": "5 · The critics: search, systems, process",
      "sentence": "Perkins: creative work is search, and it gets stuck in four ways. Ram and colleagues: the map shows creativity is possible, not how it works.",
      "quote": "Problems are hard creatively when the circumstances mask novel solutions.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Perkins 1995, 108)",
