      "notes": "- Perkins: creative systems = anything that yields adaptive novelty (minds, teams, evolution, cultures).\n- Four traps: rarity, isolation, oasis (almost good), plateau (no direction). Only isolation needs a bigger space.\n- Perkins: Boden underplays creative results from systems that never change their rules.\n- Ram et al.: the book leaves most process details unexplained — but shows an explanation is possible.\nSource: (Perkins 1995, 98–108), (Ram et al. 1995, 111)\nAsk: Last time you were stuck — oasis or plateau?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Problems are hard creatively when the circumstances mask novel solutions.",
          "cite": "Perkins 1995, 108"
        },
        {
          "text": "creative systems are any systems that over time yield adaptive novelty, that is, unexpected outcomes that are nonetheless functional in context.",
          "cite": "Perkins 1995, 98"
        },
        {
          "text": "Boden’s conception allows for but perhaps underplays the phenomenon of fundamentally creative outcomes from uncreative systems.",
          "cite": "Perkins 1995, 105"
